<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyril of Turov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1130 – c. 1182</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Kirill of Turov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyril of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Turov in the Pripyat marshes and one of the finest writers of early Rus'. His sermons for church feasts, prayers and parables, written in a rich rhetorical style modelled on Byzantine preaching, were copied widely. He is venerated as a saint in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> and honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> as an early national writer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyril-of-turov.jpg" alt="Cyril of Turov" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osip Semenovich Chirikov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyril_of_Turov_by_O.Chirikov_(Muz.ist._rel).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is certain about Cyril of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a>'s life, since none of the sources is contemporary and the dates 1130 to 1182, long accepted, are disputed by scholars such as Simon Franklin. The only biography is a short, formulaic Synaxarion Life, written no earlier than the mid-13th century and drawing heavily on hagiographic convention. It says he was born in the thriving town of Turov to wealthy parents, was exceptionally pious from a young age and entered a monastery as a young man, where he was respected for his asceticism and his learned interpretation of scripture.</p><p>He is said to have been consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a> in 1158. Usually he is thought to have died in 1182. An alternative view holds that he became a bishop only after 1182 and was a monk throughout the 1160s and 1170s; the title of bishop of Turov is also often regarded as a later addition, meant to give the author of such popular works a suitably high status.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>A body of works in several genres is attributed to Kirill: festal homilies, monastic commentaries, a few letters, a cycle of prayers and other hymns, several versions of a penitential Prayer Canon, a Canon of Olga and an abecedarian prayer. The 19th-century consensus holds that eleven of these form the core of what is called the Corpus Cyrillianum, though this is continually questioned. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> date from centuries after the supposed time of composition, and the headings call the author Kirill the monk, the philosopher, the unworthy monk and other names, so that texts might belong to any of several real Kirills.</p><p>His homilies form a cycle following the church calendar from Palm Sunday to the Sunday before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a>, and his prayers a seven-day liturgical cycle. His most popular work, in Zenkovsky's assessment, was a sermon describing spring as the symbol of the Resurrection, in which the melting of ice is compared to the dissolving of Thomas's doubt. His writing follows the Byzantine rhetorical tradition closely: Franklin counts about 370 biblical quotations and allusions in the sermons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>For centuries Kirill was prized as a writer, and his works were continuously copied and imitated, influencing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavs</a> into the 17th century. Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, among them a cathedral at Turau, are named after him, as are churches in the Belarusian diaspora in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, Brooklyn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>. His feast day in the Eastern Orthodox Church is on 28 April.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kirill of Turov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its bishop <b>Cyril of Turov</b>, who died around 1182, was one of the finest preachers and writers of early Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-turov-and-pinsk">Principality of Turov and Pinsk</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Turov, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. Died in Turov, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.07,27.73&amp;year=1163" aria-label="Turov on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5175 365 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cyril of Turov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5625 635h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5625 635h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.07,27.73&amp;year=1163">What was here in 1163? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
